From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jon Pretty <jon.pretty@propensive.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 18:16:01 +0900
Subject: Give the inline calls expanded in an inline trait's child their call
 traces

A class which extends an inline trait is given copies of the trait's
definitions, with the inline calls in them expanded, after PostTyper and
before pickling. PostTyper is where an `Inlined` node made during typing has
its call replaced by a call trace, a reference to the top-level class of the
method; the nodes made for the child class kept the calls they were expanded
from.

Such a call holds its arguments as they were. An argument which is itself the
expansion of an inline call holds the definitions of that expansion, and so
does the binding the outer node makes for the argument. The pickler therefore
wrote the same definitions twice, under two owners, sharing the trees beneath
them, and the unpickler stopped with "owner discrepancy" when it met a
definition it had already read under another owner: a class extending an
inline trait could not be read back from its own TASTy whenever a method of
the trait made an inline call on the result of another.

The definitions inlined from the trait now have the calls of their `Inlined`
nodes replaced by call traces, as PostTyper would have left them.

Written with LLM assistance (Claude Code); reviewed content.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Proscala-Patch: inlinetraittrace
